# TypeScript & React

## Separation of concerns

- Business logic lives in hooks (`use*`) or service/lib modules, never inside
  presentational components.
- Presentational components receive data and handlers via props. They own no
  fetching, no API calls, and no domain computation.
- A file that mixes JSX with data orchestration is a refactor target: extract
  `use<Feature>` and pass results down.

## Data fetching

- Fetch at the page or feature-container level. Prefer one server fetch (RSC)
  or one aggregating API response over sequential client waterfalls.
- Do not compute heavy aggregations in JS/TS loops over raw rows when a SQL
  view exists (`database-sql` if the `supabase` pack is installed).

## Types

- Strict TS; no implicit `any`. Prefer generated DB types when present.
- Run the project's typecheck after non-trivial edits.

Marketing / static landing routes may opt out of data-fetching rules — keep
them static and provider-free. Brand copy stays in consumer overlay rules.
